  "notes": "The Cheiron ballad/mid-tempo template, and the restrained end of the lane: 88–105 with ballads at 64–80, straight, backbeat on 2&4 throughout, so the session spans 64–105 with the mode on the mid-tempo centre. The harmonic signature the research names is the major key that opens on the minor vi — vi–IV–I in A on the 1999 single — plus the late whole-tone modulation, so vi–IV–I carries top weight. Three drum deltas come straight off the entry: \"kick 1 and 3 with an 8th pickup\" (anchors on 1 and 3, secondary on \"4&\"), \"brushed/soft hat 8ths in verse, 16ths plus tambourine in the chorus\" (8th-weighted base with a tambourine lane), and \"no fills across the chorus boundary, only a crash\" — which is why `fills` moves to every 8 and 16 bars, the hat rolls are near zero, and a crash lane sits in the percs. The bass follows the acoustic arpeggio rather than the kick (\"on roots and 5ths, legato, following the guitar arpeggio's harmonic rhythm\"), so it is `independent_riff` at 0.86 with the fifth as its passing tone. ⚠ The **INFERRED** parameters are the velocity softness, `syncopation` 0.18 and the perc densities — \"restrained\" is a direction, not a number. ⚠ The melody register 55–79 is a reading of the stated E3–B4 group range widened by the octave the chorus double adds.",
